1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,960
Before you judge Jensen Huang, look at what one night in 1943 cost America.

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:08,048
That spring,

3
00:00:08,088 --> 00:00:15,010
the man building the atomic bomb spent a night with a woman the government suspected of ties to the enemy.

4
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:20,610
Army agents sat outside her apartment until morning, and wrote it all down.

5
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:26,861
Eleven years later, that night was read back to him in a closed room in Washington.

6
00:00:26,901 --> 00:00:27,435
He lost.

7
00:00:27,775 --> 00:00:33,910
And it took the United States sixty-eight years to admit the process was rigged against him.

8
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:38,164
Now the man who builds the chips behind American A. I.

9
00:00:38,204 --> 00:00:41,165
has admitted an affair with a woman U. S.

10
00:00:41,205 --> 00:00:44,460
intelligence believes was recruited by China.

11
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,285
On November eighteenth, Congress wants him in a closed room too.

12
00:00:49,625 --> 00:00:52,754
So what did America actually find the last time?

13
00:00:52,794 --> 00:00:55,460
And why did it take the whole thing back?

14
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:58,535
Start with what is confirmed.

15
00:00:58,875 --> 00:01:05,174
On Sunday, November eighth, 2026, Jensen Huang emailed Nvidia's employees.

16
00:01:05,214 --> 00:01:09,285
Six words mattered. I was unfaithful to my wife.

17
00:01:09,625 --> 00:01:16,010
The same day, Nvidia's board opened an independent investigation of its own founder.

18
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:21,163
The woman is Alice Huang. No relation. She is a Chinese citizen,

19
00:01:21,203 --> 00:01:25,485
and a former executive at Megaspeed, a Singapore company.

20
00:01:25,825 --> 00:01:27,896
According to the New York Times,

21
00:01:27,936 --> 00:01:32,910
Megaspeed bought about four point eight billion dollars of Nvidia equipment.

22
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:35,835
Here is what is not confirmed. U. S.

23
00:01:35,875 --> 00:01:40,210
intelligence officials believe she was recruited by Beijing.

24
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:45,349
That is an assessment, reported by the Times and by N. B. C. News.

25
00:01:45,389 --> 00:01:48,135
Nobody has been charged with anything.

26
00:01:48,475 --> 00:01:50,621
China calls the claim baseless.

27
00:01:50,661 --> 00:01:56,685
And Nvidia's board says it has no reason to believe that any secrets were compromised.

28
00:01:57,025 --> 00:02:01,973
But one sentence is now a problem. In October, before the admission,

29
00:02:02,013 --> 00:02:08,135
Huang told Bloomberg the relationship was, quote, completely normal and not unusual.

30
00:02:08,925 --> 00:02:11,760
Why does a private affair shake a market?

31
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:19,410
Because one Wall Street analyst says Nvidia would be worth twenty-five to thirty percent less without him.

32
00:02:19,750 --> 00:02:23,445
In two days, the stock lost more than ten percent.

33
00:02:23,485 --> 00:02:26,135
Almost five hundred billion dollars.

34
00:02:26,475 --> 00:02:29,165
In Washington, the question is simpler.

35
00:02:29,205 --> 00:02:34,485
Can the man at the center of America's most strategic technology be trusted?

36
00:02:34,825 --> 00:02:39,706
America has asked that exact question before. About a different man.

37
00:02:39,746 --> 00:02:41,660
And a different technology.

38
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:47,924
Spring, 1943. Robert Oppenheimer is about to leave for Los Alamos,

39
00:02:47,964 --> 00:02:52,185
to run the most secret project in American history.

40
00:02:52,525 --> 00:02:56,695
Before he goes, he visits Jean Tatlock, in San Francisco.

41
00:02:56,735 --> 00:03:02,160
She was his first real love. She was also a member of the Communist Party.

42
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,764
He stays the night.

43
00:03:03,804 --> 00:03:08,706
He does not know that agents from Army intelligence are waiting outside,

44
00:03:08,746 --> 00:03:10,285
watching until morning.

45
00:03:10,625 --> 00:03:13,135
Their report goes to the F. B. I.

46
00:03:13,475 --> 00:03:16,785
The Bureau taps her phone. It finds nothing.

47
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:20,945
About six months later, Jean Tatlock takes her own life.

48
00:03:20,985 --> 00:03:24,460
She was never shown to have passed a single secret.

49
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:27,335
The file stays open anyway.

50
00:03:28,125 --> 00:03:31,581
Ten years pass. The bomb works. The war ends.

51
00:03:31,621 --> 00:03:36,010
Oppenheimer becomes the most famous scientist in America.

52
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:40,483
And he starts saying things powerful people do not want to hear.

53
00:03:40,523 --> 00:03:44,460
He argues against a crash program to build the hydrogen bomb.

54
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:46,157
In November 1953,

55
00:03:46,197 --> 00:03:51,992
a former congressional staff director named William Borden writes to J.

56
00:03:52,032 --> 00:03:53,060
Edgar Hoover.

57
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:59,635
His conclusion. More probably than not, Oppenheimer is an agent of the Soviet Union.

58
00:03:59,975 --> 00:04:04,957
Four days before Christmas, the chairman of the Atomic Energy Commission,

59
00:04:04,997 --> 00:04:10,391
Lewis Strauss, calls him in. His clearance is suspended. He can resign quietly,

60
00:04:10,431 --> 00:04:11,010
or fight.

61
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:16,185
He fights. Two days later, the charges arrive. Twenty-four of them.

62
00:04:16,525 --> 00:04:21,703
And there, in an official government letter, is the night from 1943.

63
00:04:21,743 --> 00:04:25,310
Intimately associated with Doctor Jean Tatlock.

64
00:04:26,100 --> 00:04:29,060
Now hold that next to 2026.

65
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:33,375
According to reporting by the New York Times and Axios,

66
00:04:33,415 --> 00:04:41,185
a classified memo about Jensen Huang's relationship circulated inside the Trump administration last spring.

67
00:04:41,525 --> 00:04:45,310
It reportedly drew on surveillance of Alice Huang.

68
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:49,324
And in May, on Air Force One, on the way to Beijing,

69
00:04:49,364 --> 00:04:55,110
Secretary of State Marco Rubio is reported to have warned the president directly.

70
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:57,707
Huang joined that trip in Alaska.

71
00:04:57,747 --> 00:05:02,510
The president says the warning did not make much of an impact on him.

72
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:08,635
In both stories, the file existed long before anyone said a word in public.

73
00:05:09,425 --> 00:05:15,787
April twelfth, 1954. A temporary government building off Constitution Avenue.

74
00:05:15,827 --> 00:05:20,360
An office turned into a courtroom. No press. No public.

75
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:24,567
Oppenheimer believes a fair hearing will clear his name.

76
00:05:24,607 --> 00:05:26,660
Here is what he does not know.

77
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,160
The F. B. I. has bugged his lawyer's office.

78
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:35,405
The prosecution reads at least two hundred seventy-three wiretap reports,

79
00:05:35,445 --> 00:05:38,860
including his conversations with his own attorneys.

80
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,731
And for the week before the hearing began, the prosecutor, Roger Robb,

81
00:05:43,771 --> 00:05:48,760
sat with the three men who would judge him, going through the F. B. I. files.

82
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:51,251
The defense was not in the room.

83
00:05:51,291 --> 00:05:55,085
The defense was never allowed to see those files at all.

84
00:05:55,875 --> 00:06:00,310
He testifies for twenty-seven hours. And Robb is ready.

85
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:03,476
He has a secret recording from 1943,

86
00:06:03,516 --> 00:06:09,128
of Oppenheimer telling a security officer a story about a spy approach.

87
00:06:09,168 --> 00:06:11,835
Parts of that story were invented.

88
00:06:12,175 --> 00:06:15,560
Robb reads it back, and asks him why he did it.

89
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:20,310
Oppenheimer's answer is five words. Because I was an idiot.

90
00:06:20,650 --> 00:06:24,510
Then comes the night. You spent the night with her, didn't you?

91
00:06:24,850 --> 00:06:26,060
Yes.

92
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,085
That is when you were working on a secret war project?

93
00:06:30,425 --> 00:06:31,635
Yes.

94
00:06:32,425 --> 00:06:34,739
Two men, seventy-two years apart.

95
00:06:34,779 --> 00:06:40,160
Each one gave a version of his private life that did not survive the record.

96
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,160
Huang said, completely normal. Weeks later, I was unfaithful.

97
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:49,456
According to Fortune, that gap, not the affair itself,

98
00:06:49,496 --> 00:06:52,860
is what the board's investigators may examine.

99
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:56,235
It was the gap that sank Oppenheimer too.

100
00:06:57,025 --> 00:06:58,723
Not everyone played along.

101
00:06:58,763 --> 00:07:03,735
The physicist Isidor Rabi told the board what he thought of the proceeding.

102
00:07:04,075 --> 00:07:09,385
We have an A-bomb, and a whole series of it. And what more do you want, mermaids?

103
00:07:09,725 --> 00:07:13,395
But Edward Teller, the champion of the hydrogen bomb,

104
00:07:13,435 --> 00:07:15,635
said the sentence that mattered.

105
00:07:15,975 --> 00:07:21,710
I would feel personally more secure if public matters would rest in other hands.

106
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:26,135
May twenty-seventh. The board votes two to one.

107
00:07:26,475 --> 00:07:32,010
Read the verdict slowly, because it is one of the strangest in American history.

108
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:37,140
It finds no evidence of disloyalty. It records his love of country.

109
00:07:37,180 --> 00:07:40,960
And it recommends that his clearance be taken anyway.

110
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:45,510
On June twenty-ninth, the commission confirms it, four to one.

111
00:07:45,850 --> 00:07:48,046
Not for leaking. Not for spying.

112
00:07:48,086 --> 00:07:51,960
For what it called fundamental defects in his character.

113
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:56,229
His consulting contract was due to expire two days later.

114
00:07:56,269 --> 00:07:59,710
They could have let it run out. They ruled anyway.

115
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:02,785
So, was America safer?

116
00:08:03,125 --> 00:08:08,241
In 1954, the United States had about three hundred nuclear weapons.

117
00:08:08,281 --> 00:08:12,935
In the decades that followed, it built some seventy thousand.

118
00:08:13,275 --> 00:08:18,335
Historians argue the hearing silenced the strongest voice for restraint.

119
00:08:18,675 --> 00:08:21,933
And the case itself did not hold up. In 1959,

120
00:08:21,973 --> 00:08:28,090
a lawyer for the commission reviewed it internally. He wrote that the system failed,

121
00:08:28,130 --> 00:08:32,635
and that a substantial injustice was done to a loyal American.

122
00:08:32,975 --> 00:08:40,142
In 1963, President Johnson handed Oppenheimer the Fermi Award. He died in 1967.

123
00:08:40,182 --> 00:08:43,335
The verdict was still on the books.

124
00:08:43,675 --> 00:08:50,635
Then, on December sixteenth, 2022, the Secretary of Energy formally vacated it.

125
00:08:50,975 --> 00:08:56,185
The order says the process was flawed, and broke the commission's own rules.

126
00:08:56,525 --> 00:09:00,688
And it says why it happened. Less out of concern for secrets.

127
00:09:00,728 --> 00:09:07,510
More out of a desire to discredit Doctor Oppenheimer in public debates over nuclear weapons policy.

128
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:12,661
That is the lesson of 1954. The private life was the tool.

129
00:09:12,701 --> 00:09:14,835
The policy was the target.

130
00:09:15,625 --> 00:09:18,560
Now look at what surrounds Jensen Huang.

131
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:22,727
For years, Washington has been fighting over one question.

132
00:09:22,767 --> 00:09:26,260
How many advanced chips should America sell to China?

133
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:32,029
Huang has argued in public for keeping that market open. Last December,

134
00:09:32,069 --> 00:09:37,035
the administration allowed Nvidia's H 200 chips to be sold there.

135
00:09:37,375 --> 00:09:40,860
Lawmakers in both parties want to restrict them.

136
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:43,200
Within days of his admission,

137
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:47,843
Senator Elizabeth Warren was urging Congress to pass those limits.

138
00:09:47,883 --> 00:09:50,235
The policy fight had a new weapon.

139
00:09:51,025 --> 00:09:55,885
None of this makes Huang another Oppenheimer. The differences are real.

140
00:09:56,225 --> 00:10:01,210
Oppenheimer held a government clearance. Huang runs a private company.

141
00:10:01,550 --> 00:10:06,028
In 1954, investigators had watched for more than a decade,

142
00:10:06,068 --> 00:10:10,235
and never claimed that a single secret left his hands.

143
00:10:10,575 --> 00:10:17,360
In 2026, nobody outside the government knows yet what, if anything, reached Beijing.

144
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:21,887
Jean Tatlock was never shown to be a spy. About Alice Huang,

145
00:10:21,927 --> 00:10:26,185
there is an intelligence assessment that no court has tested.

146
00:10:26,525 --> 00:10:29,756
If secrets were passed, that is a security case.

147
00:10:29,796 --> 00:10:32,210
And it should be made with evidence.

148
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:39,610
But 1954 leaves a test for the closed room on November eighteenth. Three questions.

149
00:10:39,950 --> 00:10:44,470
One. Is there evidence that something was actually compromised,

150
00:10:44,510 --> 00:10:46,135
or only a relationship?

151
00:10:46,475 --> 00:10:49,535
Two. Does the accused get to see the file?

152
00:10:49,875 --> 00:10:53,160
Three. Who wins the policy fight if he falls?

153
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:56,610
America skipped all three with Oppenheimer.

154
00:10:56,950 --> 00:11:00,416
It took sixty-eight years, forty-three senators,

155
00:11:00,456 --> 00:11:05,310
and every living director of Los Alamos to get the answer on paper.

156
00:11:05,650 --> 00:11:10,835
The board that judged him wrote that he was loyal. Watch what the next board writes.

157
00:11:11,175 --> 00:11:13,935
The next mirror is on your screen now.

